From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000023 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Remora <remora@localhost>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 20:40:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] vtest: rank the allocation flags — foreign import, then SCANOUT
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

bd remora-e5x.25, narrowed by bd remora-cq3. MAPPABLE|SCANOUT is unsatisfiable
on NVIDIA and we were resolving it the wrong way round; the fix for that then
overreached, and this makes the rule say what it actually means.

0001 routes MAPPABLE allocations to a Vulkan image with an explicit LINEAR
modifier so the guest can mmap, and that fixed real SurfaceFlinger crashes.
But a SCANOUT buffer is composited, so the guest later imports it as a
colour attachment — and NVIDIA refuses to create a RENDERABLE image on a
linear modifier:

  vkCreateImage FAILED ret=-11 format=37 extent=1055x1992 tiling=1000158000
                usage=0x97 modifier=explicit: 0x0 planes=1

(-11 FORMAT_NOT_SUPPORTED, format 37 R8G8B8A8_UNORM, usage 0x97 =
TRANSFER_SRC|TRANSFER_DST|SAMPLED|COLOR_ATTACHMENT|INPUT_ATTACHMENT.)
Every frame of such a surface is dropped, so the app runs perfectly and
displays PURE BLACK. Measured on an RTX 3070 Ti with Super Mario Run, whose
entire swapchain is this case: 1055x1992 AB24 arrives flags=0x3, served
LINEAR 18511 times in one launch; after, block-linear
(mod=0x300000000606014), 8 allocations, zero refusals, title screen renders.

SO SCANOUT MUST BEAT MAPPABLE — but it must not beat everything. Letting it
decide for every composited buffer broke HW video, because on bare/NVIDIA the
video path spans BOTH GPUs: c2-va decodes on the Intel xe VF (iHD,
renderD129) while SurfaceFlinger composites on NVIDIA. A surface-bound decode
session's RGBX output buffer is allocated here, on NVIDIA, and then
VPP-blitted into by Intel, which imports it by modifier:

  Output buffer info: drmFormat=XB24 modifier=0x300000000606014 fromHandle=1
  vaCreateSurfaces(DRM_PRIME_2, modifier=0x300000000606014) failed:
      resource allocation failed
  Failed to copy VA surface into output frame.
  DecodeComponent: reportError(error=14)

NVIDIA's block-linear layout is meaningless to iHD, so the import is refused,
the app gets ERROR_CODE_DECODING_FAILED and restarts the player once per
segment — audio rebuilding every ~10s, picture frozen, forever. Pluto TV
never plays a frame. The previously working layout was modifier=0x0.

So the flags rank rather than fight: LINEAR_REQUIRED (a second GPU imports
it) > SCANOUT (this GPU composites it) > MAPPABLE (the guest mmaps it). Only
the guest knows which buffers cross the device boundary, and the wire carried
no way to say so, so this adds the bit; minigbm's gbm_mesa already declares
the same contract locally by advertising BO_USE_HW_VIDEO_DECODER on the 32bpp
RGB formats with linear_metadata.

Mario Run's swapchain keeps block-linear: it is rendered INTO by NVIDIA and
read by nobody else, so it never sets the new flag.

One documented cost remains. SurfaceFlinger's screenshot path CPU-maps the
composited buffer, so with SCANOUT winning, `screencap` and the Recents
thumbnails return tiled bytes read as linear — horizontal striped noise over
the whole screen, home screen included. That path is unrelated to the video
fix and is left as-is here; VTEST_SCANOUT_LINEAR=1 still restores the old
precedence wholesale if it is needed.

Also logs every GPU allocation with its fourcc, flags and chosen routing —
that log is what made the pattern visible, and it is how you tell whether
a black app is hitting this.
---
diff --git a/vtest/vtest_protocol.h b/vtest/vtest_protocol.h
--- a/vtest/vtest_protocol.h
+++ b/vtest/vtest_protocol.h
@@ -296,6 +296,10 @@
 
 #define VCMD_ALLOC_GPU_FLAG_MAPPABLE (1u << 0) /* guest needs CPU mmap */
 #define VCMD_ALLOC_GPU_FLAG_SCANOUT (1u << 1)
+/* A SECOND GPU imports this buffer, so linear is the only layout both understand.
+ * MAPPABLE and SCANOUT both describe what the compositing GPU wants; this one says
+ * the buffer also crosses a device boundary, which outranks either (bd remora-cq3). */
+#define VCMD_ALLOC_GPU_FLAG_LINEAR_REQUIRED (1u << 2)
 
 #define VCMD_RESOURCE_ALLOC_GPU_RESP_SIZE 7
 #define VCMD_RESOURCE_ALLOC_GPU_RESP_STATUS 0 /* 0 = OK */
diff --git a/vtest/vtest_renderer.c b/vtest/vtest_renderer.c
--- a/vtest/vtest_renderer.c
+++ b/vtest/vtest_renderer.c
@@ -2544,12 +2544,58 @@
    const uint32_t drm_format = req[VCMD_RESOURCE_ALLOC_GPU_FORMAT];
    const uint32_t flags = req[VCMD_RESOURCE_ALLOC_GPU_FLAGS];
 
+   /* remora-e5x.25/cq3: the wire flags are the whole decision, and they rank —
+    * LINEAR_REQUIRED (a second GPU imports it) outranks SCANOUT (this GPU composites
+    * it) outranks MAPPABLE (the guest mmaps it). The gralloc usage bits that produced
+    * them live in the guest (minigbm gbm_mesa); these three flags are as much as the
+    * wire carries. Print the fourcc readably: these are the buffers under suspicion. */
+   fprintf(stderr, "[remora-alloc] %ux%u fourcc=%c%c%c%c flags=0x%x -> %s\n", width, height,
+           (char)(drm_format & 0xff), (char)((drm_format >> 8) & 0xff),
+           (char)((drm_format >> 16) & 0xff), (char)((drm_format >> 24) & 0xff), flags,
+           (flags & VCMD_ALLOC_GPU_FLAG_LINEAR_REQUIRED) ? "LINEAR (foreign importer)"
+           : (flags & VCMD_ALLOC_GPU_FLAG_SCANOUT)        ? "block-linear (scanout wins)"
+           : (flags & VCMD_ALLOC_GPU_FLAG_MAPPABLE)       ? "LINEAR (mappable)"
+                                                          : "block-linear");
+
    uint32_t stride = 0;
    uint64_t modifier = 0;
    uint64_t size = 0;
 
    if (!width || !height || width > 32768 || height > 32768) {
       ret = -EINVAL;
+   } else if (flags & VCMD_ALLOC_GPU_FLAG_LINEAR_REQUIRED) {
+      /*
+       * A second GPU imports this buffer, and linear is the only layout the two
+       * agree on — so this outranks both of the flags below (bd remora-cq3).
+       *
+       * On bare/NVIDIA the video path is split: c2-va decodes on the Intel xe VF
+       * (iHD, renderD129) and SurfaceFlinger composites on NVIDIA. A surface-bound
+       * decode session's RGBX output buffer is therefore allocated here, on NVIDIA,
+       * and then VPP-blitted into by Intel — which imports it with
+       * vaCreateSurfaces(DRM_PRIME_2, modifier). NVIDIA's block-linear modifier is
+       * meaningless to iHD and the import is refused outright:
+       *
+       *   Output buffer info: drmFormat=XB24 modifier=0x300000000606014 fromHandle=1
+       *   vaCreateSurfaces(DRM_PRIME_2, modifier=0x300000000606014) failed:
+       *       resource allocation failed
+       *   Failed to copy VA surface into output frame.
+       *   DecodeComponent: reportError(error=14)
+       *
+       * The app sees ERROR_CODE_DECODING_FAILED and restarts the player once per
+       * segment: audio rebuilds every ~10s and the picture never moves.
+       *
+       * These buffers are also SCANOUT (they are composited), so without this flag
+       * they fall into the branch below and get block-linear. That is the regression
+       * this rule closes: SCANOUT is the right default for a buffer only NVIDIA
+       * touches, but it must not decide for one Intel has to read.
+       *
+       * Note the guest already declares the contract on its side — minigbm's
+       * gbm_mesa combos advertise BO_USE_HW_VIDEO_DECODER on the 32bpp RGB formats
+       * with linear_metadata. Until this flag it had no way to say so on the wire,
+       * where only MAPPABLE and SCANOUT existed.
+       */
+      ret = vtest_gpu_alloc_gpu_linear(width, height, drm_format, &stride, &modifier,
+                                       &size, &fd);
    } else if (flags & VCMD_ALLOC_GPU_FLAG_MAPPABLE) {
       /*
        * Serve CPU-mappable gralloc buffers as a GPU image, not as memfd+udmabuf.
@@ -2572,8 +2618,45 @@
        * Set VTEST_MAPPABLE_UDMABUF=1 to restore upstream's behaviour.
        */
       const char *want_udmabuf = getenv("VTEST_MAPPABLE_UDMABUF");
+      const char *keep_linear = getenv("VTEST_SCANOUT_LINEAR");
       if (want_udmabuf && *want_udmabuf == '1') {
          ret = vtest_gpu_alloc_cpu(width, height, drm_format, &stride, &size, &fd);
+      } else if ((flags & VCMD_ALLOC_GPU_FLAG_SCANOUT) &&
+                 !(keep_linear && *keep_linear == '1')) {
+         /*
+          * MAPPABLE|SCANOUT is unsatisfiable on NVIDIA, and SCANOUT is the half that
+          * must win (bd remora-e5x.25).
+          *
+          * MAPPABLE forces an explicit LINEAR modifier so the guest can mmap. But a
+          * SCANOUT buffer is composited, so the guest later imports it as a colour
+          * attachment — and NVIDIA refuses to create a RENDERABLE image on a linear
+          * modifier: "vkCreateImage FAILED ret=-11 (FORMAT_NOT_SUPPORTED) format=37
+          * usage=0x97 modifier=explicit: 0x0". Every frame of such a surface is
+          * dropped, so the app runs perfectly and displays PURE BLACK.
+          *
+          * vtest_gpu_alloc_image()'s own non-linear branch already encodes the rule
+          * this restores: it enumerates the driver's modifiers, demands
+          * COLOR_ATTACHMENT|SAMPLED and explicitly excludes modifier 0. The MAPPABLE
+          * test simply ran first and took precedence over it.
+          *
+          * Measured on an RTX 3070 Ti with Super Mario Run, whose whole swapchain is
+          * this case: 1055x1992 AB24 arrived flags=0x3 and was served LINEAR 18511
+          * times in one launch, every one refused; the full-screen 3760x1992 AB24
+          * SCANOUT-only buffer (flags=0x2) took the block-linear path and composited
+          * fine throughout. That contrast is the whole bug.
+          *
+          * The cost is the CPU map for these buffers. This rule originally claimed
+          * nothing should need it, because a composited surface is produced and
+          * consumed by the GPU. Two things do, and both broke (bd remora-cq3):
+          * SurfaceFlinger's screenshot path CPU-maps the composited buffer, so
+          * `screencap` and the Recents thumbnails came back as tiled bytes read as
+          * linear — horizontal striped noise over the whole screen; and a decode
+          * output buffer is read by the OTHER GPU. The second is what
+          * LINEAR_REQUIRED above now carves out; the screenshot path still pays the
+          * cost. VTEST_SCANOUT_LINEAR=1 restores the old precedence wholesale.
+          */
+         ret = vtest_gpu_alloc_gpu(width, height, drm_format, &stride, &modifier,
+                                   &size, &fd);
       } else {
          ret = vtest_gpu_alloc_gpu_linear(width, height, drm_format, &stride, &modifier,
                                           &size, &fd);
